From: Jan Procházka <pjanv42@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] llvm: synchronize target initialization

Backport of ziglang/zig 75044cb04cc67454db98ed7c054081806c3830c6
(https://codeberg.org/ziglang/zig/commit/75044cb04cc67454db98ed7c054081806c3830c6,
closes https://codeberg.org/ziglang/zig/issues/36490) to the 0.15.2 tag
(e4cbd752c8c05f131051f8c873cff7823177d7d3). The upstream commit is kept
verbatim in docs/upstream/zig/.

LLVM's target registry is a process-global linked list with no locking of
its own. Concurrent sub-compilations (compiler_rt, libzigc, ubsan_rt, libc++)
each call initializeLLVMTarget on their own thread, so one can look its
target up while another is still registering, and LLVMGetTargetFromTriple
then reports "No available targets are compatible with triple".

Adapted, not cherry-picked: master's version takes an `std.Io` and locks an
`std.Io.Mutex`; 0.15.2 predates `std.Io`, so this locks a `std.Thread.Mutex`
and keeps the one-argument signature. Every 0.15.2 caller
(src/codegen/llvm.zig emit, src/link/Lld.zig linkAsArchive, src/main.zig
cmdDumpLlvmInts) is therefore unchanged. Apply with -p1 inside zig/.

diff --git a/src/codegen/llvm.zig b/src/codegen/llvm.zig
index bd2c7bd0..767bb810 100644
--- a/src/codegen/llvm.zig
+++ b/src/codegen/llvm.zig
@@ -13000,7 +13000,14 @@ fn constraintAllowsRegister(constraint: []const u8) bool {
     } else return false;
 }
 
+var target_registry_mutex: std.Thread.Mutex = .{};
+
 pub fn initializeLLVMTarget(arch: std.Target.Cpu.Arch) void {
+    // Repeated initialization is safe, as targets which have already been registered will be skipped.
+    // It is however the client's responsibility to synchronize registry access.
+    target_registry_mutex.lock();
+    defer target_registry_mutex.unlock();
+
     switch (arch) {
         .aarch64, .aarch64_be => {
             llvm.LLVMInitializeAArch64Target();
